  "body": {
    "id": "lem:lipschitz-lower-pair",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "Specialize \\(\\texttt{def:lipschitz-lower-pair}\\) to \\(\\alpha=1\\) and \\(\\kappa=2\\). For every \\(0<h\\leq1/4\\), both members of \\(P_h^{\\mathrm{pair}}=(P_{0,h},P_{1,h})\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal P_{1,1,1,2,L}\\), and \\[|\\theta(P_{1,h})-\\theta(P_{0,h})|=h^2/8,\\qquad \\operatorname{TV}(P_{0,h},P_{1,h})=h^4/96,\\qquad \\operatorname{KL}(P_{0,h},P_{1,h})\\leq h^5/240.\\] For \\(n\\geq4^5\\), \\(h=n^{-1/5}\\) gives the clean two-point lower rate \\(n^{-2/5}\\), while \\(h=1/4\\) covers the remaining finite set of sample sizes after adjusting the constant. With \\(h=\\{\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)\\}^{1/4}/4\\), the explicit contaminants satisfy \\(R_h=(1-\\epsilon)P_{0,h}+\\epsilon Q_{0,h}=(1-\\epsilon)P_{1,h}+\\epsilon Q_{1,h}\\), and the clean targets are separated by a constant multiple of \\(\\{\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)\\}^{1/2}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "h",
      "P_h_pair",
      "P_0_h",
      "P_1_h",
      "Q_0_h",
      "Q_1_h",
      "R_h",
      "mathcal_P_clean",
      "L",
      "theta",
      "TV",
      "KL",
      "n",
      "epsilon"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "lem:lipschitz-lower-pair-all-kappa",
      "def:lipschitz-lower-pair"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Apply \\(\\texttt{lem:lipschitz-lower-pair-all-kappa}\\) with \\(\\alpha=1\\) and \\(\\kappa=2\\). Its three exact formulas become\n\\[\n\\frac{h^2}{8},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{h^4}{8\\cdot3\\cdot4}=\\frac{h^4}{96},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{h^5}{4\\cdot3\\cdot4\\cdot5}=\\frac{h^5}{240}.\n\\]\nFor \\(n\\geq4^5\\), the admissible choice \\(h=n^{-1/5}\\) has product divergence at most \\(n h^5/240=1/240\\), while its target separation is \\(n^{-2/5}/8\\). For \\(2\\leq n<4^5\\), the choice \\(h=1/4\\) has product divergence smaller than \\(1/240\\) and a fixed positive target separation; since this is a finite range and \\(n^{-2/5}\\leq1\\), decreasing the lower-bound constant covers it. The elementary two-point inequality proved in \\(\\texttt{lem:lipschitz-lower-pair-all-kappa}\\) therefore gives the stated clean lower rate. For contamination, the all-\\(\\kappa\\) lemma chooses \\(h=4^{-1}\\{\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)\\}^{1/4}\\), exactly as here, proves equality of the two observed mixtures, and gives target separation \\(128^{-1}\\{\\epsilon/(1-\\epsilon)\\}^{1/2}\\). Thus every assertion of the stated \\(\\kappa=2\\) lemma follows.",
    "justification": "This retained quadratic specialization is an exact numerical check of the generalized pair.",
    "gap": "Its constants are not imported from generic testing theory; they arise from a pair inside the exact declared class.",
    "consumer": "It preserves the original kappa equal to two calibration as a transparent consistency check.",
